  "selected": [
    {
      "id": "common-comparison-mask",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/common-comparison-mask.md",
      "sha256": "bbb98200f52b4a692ce097704b8c438179a795a44bec6b856c03cf31931af4ea",
      "reason": "The bias, RMSE and pattern correlation against WOA23 must be computed only on cells where both model and WOA23 have data, using one common mask applied consistently to all three models, and the WOA23 land-sea mask must be kept out of comparisons that do not involve WOA23.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Bring all three models and WOA23 onto the 1° grid, build one mask with `build_comparison_mask` and apply it with `.where(mask)` to every field before any bias, RMSE or pattern correlation; `ocean_only=True` is right here because WOA23 is the reference. Report the mask's cell counts beside the metrics.",
        "Take the means behind the bias from `compute_difference_metrics` (`mean_data`, `mean_reference`) over that single shared sample and report them with the bias; not global means each computed over its own ocean."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "historical-ssp245-member-continuity",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "pitfalls/historical-ssp245-member-continuity.md",
      "sha256": "02341c56015c6296db14585b4561fc32ca6b4e52e2264bec50eee6746c1d55f6",
      "reason": "The requested caveat about ensemble members applies directly because local CESM2 ssp245 continues r10i1p1f1 while local CESM2 historical is r1i1p1f1, so its historical-to-ssp245 delta mixes forced change with internal variability between members.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "CESM2 historical is r1i1p1f1 while its ssp245 is r10i1p1f1 (parent_variant_label historical r10i1p1f1, and historical r10 is not in the archive): its delta SSS and slope ratio contain the difference in internal variability between two members — state this. GFDL-ESM4 and MPI-ESM1-2-LR pair r1 with r1 and are continuous.",
        "Do not fit one trend across the 2014/2015 join for CESM2; each segment gets its own fitted line, gap left empty."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices.md",
      "sha256": "e0afb5c187aca1e075f9549153b1e896320bb0539544f66d3293fab4b4b77c92",
      "reason": "The SSS bias and the historical-versus-ssp245 change must be interpreted with explicit baselines, since an anomaly or difference is defined relative to its baseline and comparison to the WOA23 1971-2000 normal keeps the observed bias while removing a model's own climatological bias.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "State the climatology and its baseline with the bias: it is against the WOA23 1971-2000 normal. Two series built on different baselines differ by an offset (one constant per calendar month) even when their variability is identical."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "serial-correlation-and-significance",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/serial-correlation-and-significance.md",
      "sha256": "fe11bd008257a589f44e369faba5fa31ab3cf9ad99280e88c763ac45d2b0e917",
      "reason": "The requested linear trends, 95% confidence intervals, R-squared values and p<0.05 stippling require accounting for strong autocorrelation in monthly and annual ocean series rather than naive sample sizes.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "`fit_1D_trend` and `fit_trend_map` apply an effective sample size along time by default and return the unadjusted values alongside; quote the corrected ones. Fit `method='linear'` (or `'theil_sen'`) explicitly — automatic model selection answers a different question.",
        "Call a trend significant only if its 95 per cent interval excludes zero; draw the slope everywhere and stipple the significant cells rather than blanking the insignificant ones."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-weighted-means-and-integrals",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-weighted-means-and-integrals.md",
      "sha256": "89de52676ae54db8f5897bc77a08c78de3f776696db0020935a38b790c302cbe",
      "reason": "Global SSS means and basin means need the appropriate area weighting over all ocean cells, since OMIP global means are weighted means and surface means are area-weighted over the top level.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "A global mean SSS is Σ SSS·`areacello` / Σ `areacello` over valid ocean cells only, with the weight restricted to the same cells as the valid data (NaN-aware); `compute_basic_stat` with `op='area_weighted_mean'`. Unweighted `mean()` over a curvilinear or latitude–longitude grid is wrong.",
        "`compute_difference_metrics` weights by area by default; `analyze_correlation` needs `weighting='area'` for a pattern correlation between two maps."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "basin-and-region-definitions",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/basin-and-region-definitions.md",
      "sha256": "d0d90c32fab9f6e0a39d4f769a4e902e6412b2ad1b09d9c7d19f6bb3aa426611",
      "reason": "The requested Atlantic, Indian and Pacific basin means depend on which basin partition is used, so the WOA23 basin-mask definition and the treatment of marginal seas, Southern Ocean and Arctic must be stated.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Basin statistics use the WOA23 basin mask (`select_basin`, `load_basin_mask`), not the OMIP `basin` field (not in the local archive); `include_marginal_seas=True` folds marginal seas into their parent ocean — state that choice, and compare basin means only under the same mask.",
        "For a surface field the surface labels (`depth_dependent=False`) are what a named ocean usually means; marginal seas present in the observational mask may have no model counterpart (CESM2 treats the Caspian Sea as a lake, not in the ocean model)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-grid-labels",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "datasets/cmip6-grid-labels.md",
      "sha256": "e349f9e305621b6c10b35cccd0490da521cc7e3ba08bfb53890479f81024d117",
      "reason": "The models are a mix of native gn fields and GFDL-ESM4's conservatively regridded gr output, which affects masks, conservation and which operations are exact, including putting curvilinear fields on the WOA23 1-degree grid for comparison and display.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "GFDL-ESM4 is `gr`: its SSS and `areacello` are already regridded to the 1x1 degree archive grid with a regridded land–sea mask, so state that its \"native\" numbers are archive-grid numbers.",
        "Resolution differs by about a factor of 2.5 between CESM2 (100 km) and MPI (250 km); features narrower than the coarse grid are not comparable in amplitude — a caveat on the bias maps."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-so-practical-salinity",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-so-practical-salinity.md",
      "sha256": "96f2763290996040d675ca6e8f997ea29fa320b8f6e6793bf2c6fcb739345e19",
      "reason": "CMIP6 salinity is compared as Practical Salinity in psu whatever the model thermodynamics, so the SSS values and biases must be reported in psu without treating them as Absolute Salinity.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Model `so` and WOA23 salinity are both practical salinity: difference them directly in psu with no unit conversion (\"0.001\", \"1\" and \"psu\" denote the same numbers).",
        "Do not multiply `so` by 0.001 or 1000 because of the units string; the stored values are already of order 35."
      ]
    }
